</person-group><article-title>Insights into Fabrication Mechanism of Pure Copper Thin Wall Components by Selective Infrared Laser Melting</article-title><source>Rapid Prototyp. J.</source><year>2019</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>1388</fpage><lpage>1397</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1108/RPJ-06-2018-0143</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f001" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Illustration of the melt pool geometry and the global and local coordinates.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g001.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g001.jpg?><?image-size 28921?><?image-md5 471bdb629331f48c8211b9abf84f942d?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1614?><?image-original-width 2447?><?image-scaled-height 461?><?image-scaled-width 699?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/471bdb629331/materials-19-02487-g001.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g001.gif?><?thumb-size 5369?><?thumb-md5 8f8b0ebfd13ee3d35ebae9f140cf9728?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 121?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/8f8b0ebfd13e/materials-19-02487-g001.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f002" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Rosenthal-based workflow for estimating melt pool dimensions and constructing LOF process windows for PBF-LB/M. Here, k is the thermal conductivity, α is the thermal diffusivity, T<sub>m</sub> is the melting temperature, T<sub>0</sub> is the initial powder-bed temperature, ε is the laser absorptivity, H is the hatching distance, L is the layer thickness, Q is the laser power, Q<sub>min</sub> and Q<sub>max</sub> are the minimum and maximum laser power considered in the process window, <inline-formula><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="mm62" overflow="scroll"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>v</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula> is the scanning speed, <inline-formula><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="mm63" overflow="scroll"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>v</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula><sub>min</sub> and <inline-formula><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="mm64" overflow="scroll"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>v</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula><sub>max</sub> are the minimum and maximum scanning speed considered in the process window, N = 2πk(t<sub>m</sub> − t<sub>0</sub>)/(Qε), M = <inline-formula><mml:math xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="mm65" overflow="scroll"><mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>v</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></inline-formula>/(2α), r is the radial distance from the heat source to the melt pool boundary at the point of maximum width, y is the melt pool half width, W is the melt pool width, and D is the melt pool depth.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g002.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g002.jpg?><?image-size 93883?><?image-md5 795ac151da4d160263ebabccb5733b40?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2456?><?image-original-width 2365?><?image-scaled-height 818?><?image-scaled-width 788?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/795ac151da4d/materials-19-02487-g002.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g002.gif?><?thumb-size 6215?><?thumb-md5 b6173fb1504c58b7a642d05af73ed6dd?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 104?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/b6173fb1504c/materials-19-02487-g002.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f003" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Surface temperature distribution for copper, H13 steel, AlSi10Mg, and Ti-6Al-4V for a PBF-LB/M process using a laser power of 200 W and a scanning speed of 500 mm/s.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g003.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g003.jpg?><?image-size 82040?><?image-md5 42ba5536b9ae8d574945aa4b4e3e43c1?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3008?><?image-original-width 3618?><?image-scaled-height 601?><?image-scaled-width 723?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/42ba5536b9ae/materials-19-02487-g003.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g003.gif?><?thumb-size 6731?><?thumb-md5 1a84a501d3b104dd2ade5dadad1684ca?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 83?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/1a84a501d3b1/materials-19-02487-g003.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f004" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Process window for the PBF-LB/M of copper at a layer thickness of 30 μm for NIR laser (<bold>A</bold>) and green laser (<bold>B</bold>).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g004.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g004.jpg?><?image-size 146210?><?image-md5 8d7ac13b60625d3939b8e6bccdab79f3?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2075?><?image-original-width 3290?><?image-scaled-height 461?><?image-scaled-width 731?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/8d7ac13b6062/materials-19-02487-g004.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g004.gif?><?thumb-size 7099?><?thumb-md5 08d86c69b17d7e6b69515dc53e67660e?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 79?><?thumb-scaled-width 126?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/08d86c69b17d/materials-19-02487-g004.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f005" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Melt pool size comparisons for PBF-LB/M on copper with laser powers of 200 W, 400 W, and 600 W and a scanning speed of 400 mm/s. The inputs were copper’s thermal conductivity, a fixed scanning speed of 400 mm/s, an absorptivity of 0.35, and laser powers of 200 W, 400 W, and 600 W.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g005.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g005.jpg?><?image-size 60675?><?image-md5 831c9b9c86810ed500e5a397527a30af?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1820?><?image-original-width 3305?><?image-scaled-height 404?><?image-scaled-width 734?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/831c9b9c8681/materials-19-02487-g005.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g005.gif?><?thumb-size 7755?><?thumb-md5 a5467e153d7cab8ca9f9c49421ef693c?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 145?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/a5467e153d7c/materials-19-02487-g005.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f006" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Melt pool depth comparisons for PBF-LB/M of copper with laser powers of 200 W, 400 W, and 600 W and a scanning speed of 400 mm/s.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g006.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g006.jpg?><?image-size 88827?><?image-md5 c5d770b1093be531e1a297bdc2761615?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2480?><?image-original-width 3004?><?image-scaled-height 620?><?image-scaled-width 751?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/c5d770b1093b/materials-19-02487-g006.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g006.gif?><?thumb-size 7364?><?thumb-md5 3bb0eb17a60c99bd28f39b20bf177570?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 83?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/3bb0eb17a60c/materials-19-02487-g006.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f007" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Melt pool size comparisons for PBF-LB/M of copper with a laser power of 400 W and scanning speeds of 100 mm/s, 500 mm/s, and 900 mm/s.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g007.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g007.jpg?><?image-size 87107?><?image-md5 a424b94df058eba5d152d031aac05345?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2097?><?image-original-width 3087?><?image-scaled-height 524?><?image-scaled-width 771?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/a424b94df058/materials-19-02487-g007.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g007.gif?><?thumb-size 7464?><?thumb-md5 e01d33ea936100af18cc033f12cd6990?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 79?><?thumb-scaled-width 117?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/e01d33ea9361/materials-19-02487-g007.gif?></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="materials-19-02487-f008" orientation="portrait"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Process window for PBF-LB/M of copper (30 μm layer thickness) overlaid with literature-reported parameter sets. Data in disagreement with the process window are circled in red [<xref rid="B17-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>,<xref rid="B36-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>,<xref rid="B51-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">51</xref>,<xref rid="B52-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">52</xref>,<xref rid="B53-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">53</xref>,<xref rid="B54-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">54</xref>,<xref rid="B55-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">55</xref>,<xref rid="B56-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">56</xref>,<xref rid="B57-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">57</xref>].</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="materials-19-02487-g008.jpg"><?image-name materials-19-02487-g008.jpg?><?image-size 234394?><?image-md5 cea9dcceab6305013be0e8ab7447f628?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3201?><?image-original-width 3296?><?image-scaled-height 711?><?image-scaled-width 732?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/cea9dcceab63/materials-19-02487-g008.jpg?><?thumb-name materials-19-02487-g008.gif?><?thumb-size 7460?><?thumb-md5 f19e0fe9b7dbc77bf94d002008a7d2ec?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 97?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/1b73/13302085/f19e0fe9b7db/materials-19-02487-g008.gif?></graphic></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="materials-19-02487-t001" orientation="portrait"><object-id pub-id-type="pii">materials-19-02487-t001_Table 1</object-id><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Magnitude of the non-negligible term in Equation (1) for highly conductive metals.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Material</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right-Hand-Side Term in Equation (1)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cu</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.992</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ag</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.997</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Al</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.973</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Au</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.996</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="materials-19-02487-t002" orientation="portrait"><object-id pub-id-type="pii">materials-19-02487-t002_Table 2</object-id><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Material constants for copper.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Property</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Symbol</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Value</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Thermal conductivity</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">400 [<xref rid="B31-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">W/m·K</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Density</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ρ</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8933 [<xref rid="B30-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">kg/m<sup>3</sup></td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Specific heat capacity</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">c<sub>p</sub></td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">385 [<xref rid="B30-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">J/kg·K</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Thermal diffusivity</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">α</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.16 × 10<sup>−4</sup></td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">m<sup>2</sup>/s</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Melting temperature</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T<sub>m</sub></td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1358 [<xref rid="B30-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">K</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ambient temperature</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T<sub>0</sub></td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">303 [<xref rid="B32-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>,<xref rid="B33-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>,<xref rid="B34-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">K</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Laser absorptivity (NIR)</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ε</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.27–0.33 [<xref rid="B22-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>,<xref rid="B35-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>,<xref rid="B36-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>,<xref rid="B37-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N/A</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Laser absorptivity (green)</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ε</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.72–0.88 [<xref rid="B22-materials-19-02487" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]</td><td align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N/A</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="materials-19-02487-t003" orientation="portrait"><object-id pub-id-type="pii">materials-19-02487-t003_Table 3</object-id><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Absorptivity sensitivity.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid thin;border-bottom:solid thin" rowspan="1" colspan="1">#</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-top:solid